// PROLOG TUTORIAL 2 — rules, negation, disjunction.
//
// Tutorial 1 asked questions against facts. This one is about the
// three moves that turn facts into REASONING. Open rules.pl beside
// this scene — every rule used here is a few lines you can read.
//
//   RULE:        head :- body.   "head is true IF the body is"
//                with(A, B, TN) :- present_at(A, R, T), present_at(B, R, T), ...
//                — a rule is a named conjunction with variables.
//   NEGATION:    \+ goal   "goal CANNOT BE PROVEN". In general Prolog
//                that's a shaky stand-in for false — but our facts
//                derive exhaustively from geometry, so unprovable
//                really does mean not-true-in-this-world.
//   DISJUNCTION: (a ; b)   "a OR b" — Prolog tries a, then b.
//
// Everything here answers against ONE world — statement order never
// matters. To experiment, EDIT the scene (walk bob to the shed, say)
// and watch every answer recompute at once.

view top
theme clay
time now 0

room kitchen color(#c8a05a)
room garden east-of(kitchen) color(#7fa06a)
room shed east-of(garden) color(#8a7a5a)
room unknown size(4 2.5 4) at(-6 0 -6) color(black)

set people
cylinder alice r(0.22) h(1.6) at(kitchen -1 0) color(#c8451f)
cylinder bob r(0.24) h(1.7) at(kitchen 1 1) color(#3d7fa6)
cylinder carol r(0.22) h(1.6) at(unknown 0 0) color(#7a5a8a)
cylinder dana r(0.22) h(1.6) at(garden 0 -1) color(#1f7a6d)
end

// --- 1. NEGATION AS FAILURE --------------------------------------------
// "Is bob NOT in the garden?" \+ succeeds because no fact places him
// there — and absence is meaningful in a closed world.
?- \+ present_at(bob, garden, 0)
// Careful, the classic surprise: \+ with an UNBOUND variable asks "is
// there NO X at all such that..." — bob IS somewhere, so this is no.
// (It does NOT mean "list the rooms without bob".)
?- \+ present_at(bob, X, 0)

// --- 2. DISJUNCTION, and binding through it ----------------------------
// "Alice is in the kitchen or the garden — which?" The binding that
// survives tells you which branch was true.
?- (R = kitchen ; R = garden), present_at(alice, R, 0)

// --- 3. THE UNKNOWN ROOM: stated ignorance -----------------------------
// carol is parked in `unknown` — the convention for "we can't place
// her". The rules treat that room specially, and you can read it in
// their source: with/3 refuses to call two things "together" there...
?- present_at(carol, unknown, 0)
?- with(carol, alice, now)
// ...and could_be_at/3 reads unknown as "could be ANYWHERE" — compare:
?- could_be_at(carol, shed, 0)
?- could_be_at(alice, shed, 0)

// --- 4. ANATOMY OF A REAL RULE: exactly_one ----------------------------
// "EXACTLY one person in the room" — how do you say exactly, in logic?
// A witness plus a DOUBLE NEGATION: "X is there, and there is no OTHER
// Y also there." Read exactly_one/4 in rules.pl — it's five lines.
// The kitchen fails it (alice AND bob), the garden passes (dana):
?- exactly_one(people, kitchen, now, X)
?- exactly_one(people, garden, now, X)

// --- 5. put the moves together ------------------------------------------
// "someone is alone in some room, and it isn't bob" — conjunction, a
// rule, a negation, two variables binding at once. TWO answers come
// back, and the first is a lesson in itself: carol is "alone" in the
// UNKNOWN room — exactly_one doesn't special-case the staging room
// (with/3 does; read both in rules.pl). Rules know only what their
// source says. If that answer bothers you, the fix is a rule, not a
// wish — try adding R \= unknown to the goal.
?- exactly_one(people, R, now, X), \+ X = bob

check in(alice kitchen) at(now)
check in(dana garden) at(now)
